	abstract = {The appeal of MRI for brain research lies in its capacity to non-invasively measure biological changes but, to reliably represent the ground truth, measurements rely on image quality. Image quality metrics (IQMs) can be generated by MRIQC1, a popular tool that automates quality prediction in brain MR images. Until now, MRIQC’s workflows relied on a single reference space while the software tools and algorithms were developed for use with adult human MRI and make assumptions that do not extend to other species and populations (e.g., babies), so MRIQC’s generalisability was limited.},
